 ISSISSIPPI SECRETARY OF STATE v. \nREPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE et al. \n
 certiorari to the united states court of appeals for \nthe fth circuit 
 \nNo. 24–1260. Argued March 23\, 2026—Decided June 29\, 2026 \nThe fed
 eral election-day statutes set the day for the “election” of Repre-\ns
 entatives\, Senators\, and the President on a Tuesday in November. \nSee 3
  U. S. C. § 1\, 2 U. S. C. §§ 1\, 7. Mississippi permits certain resi-\
 ndents\, such as college students away from home and senior citizens\, to 
 \nvote in federal elections by absentee ballot. Miss. Code Ann. § 23–15
 – \n713. Mississippi is one of roughly 30 States that count at least som
 e \nabsentee ballots mailed by election day but received afterward. Ab-\ns
 entee voters in Mississippi may dispatch their ballots by mail or com-\nmo
 n carrier\, and all absentee ballots must be “postmarked on or before \n
 the date of the election and received by the registrar no more than fve \n
 (5) business days after the election.” §§ 23–15–637(1)(a)\, (3). \
 nIn 2024\, the Republican National Committee\, the Mississippi Republi-\nc
 an Party\, and various individuals sued the Mississippi secretary of state
  \nand several election offcials\, contending that federal law prevents Mi
 s-\nsissippi from counting absentee ballots received after election day. A
 c-\ncording to plaintiffs\, the election-day statutes use the word “elec
 tion” \nto refer to two acts—ballot casting and ballot receipt—so by
  setting the \nday for the “election\,” t\nhttps://www.supremecourt.go
 v/opinions/25pdf/609us2r65_g314.pdf
